{"images":[],"manifest":{"budgetTokens":60000,"countMethod":"proxy","model":"gpt-6-luna","packSha":"36c92f8494c6e5a37f28b23e60602cb61a490f32720cfcc5163304debe26adfa","packTokens":19902,"recipeVersion":"1","redactions":0,"sections":[{"id":"tpl","tokens":1199,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"tpl-end","tokens":49,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"approved-screens-rules","tokens":217,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"contract","tokens":117,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"acs","tokens":8425,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"approved-screens","tokens":3311,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"stubs","tokens":231,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"previous-tests","tokens":3966,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"pointers","tokens":32,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"failures","tokens":1814,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"task","tokens":409,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"recap","tokens":35,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"}],"stage":"author-tests"},"pointers":[{"path":"contracts/openapi.yaml","reason":"the locked API contract; the client and test handlers in lib/api are generated from it"}],"system":"You write black-box acceptance tests for a TypeScript web app (React, with the factory's component kit), one test per acceptance criterion, before the feature exists.\nRules:\n- Put the tests in tests/ at the repo root, as *.test.ts files (*.test.tsx for a screen test), with vitest (import { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\"). The \"@/\" import alias points at the app's source root.\n- Test through public surfaces only: exported functions and modules, route handlers, and a screen as a person uses it.\n- Name each acceptance test exactly AC_<req>_<n>_<Words> (the it() title) for acceptance criterion AC-<req>.<n>, e.g. it(\"AC_1_2_ReturnsNotFoundWhenOrderMissing\", ...). Name characterisation tests CHAR_<Words>. The factory finds tests by these titles.\n- New APIs exist as stubs that throw new Error(\"not implemented\"); tests must import them and fail for now.\n- Also write characterisation tests for existing behaviour next to the change that must NOT change; those must pass today. A new app has little of its own: write none when there is nothing to keep.\n- Don't change production code, package.json, tsconfig or the vitest config.\n- You may run \"npx tsc --noEmit\" to check the tests compile. Don't run the tests: the factory runs them itself in its test lab.\n- Test each criterion at its level: \"unit\" criteria call the module directly; this web app has no API of its own: the API is a separate service with locked tests of its own, and here it is reached only through the generated client. An \"api\" or \"job\" criterion is tested as what this app does with the API's answer: answer the call with the contract's test handlers or a mocked client, then assert what the screen or the web code does. Never test a rule only the server can keep (what is stored, what survives a restart, seeded records, the order a status may change in, what bad data is answered with) by calling the generated client and expecting the rule to hold: nothing in this repo may implement it. Never test the generated client itself. Skip \"manual\" criteria: a person checks those.\n- Outside systems (an email or SMS provider, a payment service, another company's API, a file store) are never called by a test: the test lab has no network. Put a stand-in behind the seam the code reaches the system through: replace the system's module with vi.mock. The stand-in records every call it gets; assert from that record what was sent (to whom, with which values, how many times), and make the stand-in fail where a criterion is about the system failing. Use a stand-in the tests already have for that system before you write one. A stand-in is test code: keep it with the tests, where it is locked with them.\n- A \"ui\" criterion gets a screen test: a tests/*.test.tsx file whose FIRST line is \"// @vitest-environment jsdom\". Render the screen's container (components/screens/<s-id>/container.tsx, the page with its data and behaviour) with render() from \"@testing-library/react\", and act as a person does with userEvent from \"@testing-library/user-event\".\n- Assert what a person sees and can do: find things by role and name (screen.getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Save\" })), by label (getByLabelText) or by the words on the page, never by class name, test id or component internals. Use findBy... for what appears after data loads.\n- There is no router, no server and no network in a screen test. Navigation is recorded: import { nav, at } from \"./support/screen\" (from tests/), call at(\"/orders/7\", { tab: \"paid\" }) before render() to set the address, and assert where the page went with nav.went (e.g. expect(nav.went).toContain(\"/orders/7\")).\n- Stand in for the API with vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", ...) or by mocking the generated client module (vi.mock), and answer with data in the shape the contract gives. Give each test its own answers; undo them in afterEach (vi.unstubAllGlobals(), vi.restoreAllMocks()).\n- A screen test proves behaviour on the screen: what is shown for which data, what a click or a typed value does, which message appears. It cannot judge looks (colour, spacing, a width): don't assert styles or class names.\n- Don't edit tests/support/ or the vitest config; the factory owns them. Don't use snapshots.\n\n\n- Give no HTTP probes: the endpoints are served by the API, not by this app. Return an empty list of probes.\nReturn the list of tests you wrote (acId, file, method name).\n\nThe approved-screens section lists the screens a person approved for these requirements: route, states, and the exact words on them (title, buttons, field labels, column headers, empty, error, success and validation messages, toasts; \"change\" for a design note). Where a criterion is about what the user sees or is told, take the expected values from there, word for word, and do not invent other wording. A criterion with no screen there is tested as before. For each test that opens one of the approved screens, also return that screen's id as \"screen\" (the screen the test renders, which can be another than the one its requirement is listed under): the test is then held at the task that builds that screen. Leave it out for a test that opens no screen.\n\nAPI CONTRACT (locked: contracts/openapi.yaml). The app talks to the API only through the generated client in lib/api/client.ts; never write fetch calls or response types by hand, and never edit lib/api or contracts/openapi.yaml. Tests that need the API start the generated handlers from lib/api/client.msw.ts with msw's setupServer (msw/node); they answer with the contract's examples, so assert on that data.","tools":[],"user":"<artifact id=\"acs\" kind=\"acceptance-criteria\" sha=\"\">\n[\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-1\",\n  \"given\": \"The sign-in screen is displayed and the authentication service accepts the submitted credentials and returns the user and permissions\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-1.1\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen shows the returned operational landing page and navigation actions allowed by the returned permissions\",\n  \"when\": \"The user submits an email address and password\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-1\",\n  \"given\": \"The sign-in screen is displayed and the authentication service refuses the submitted credentials\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-1.2\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen shows the returned error message and remains on the sign-in page\",\n  \"when\": \"The user submits an email address and password\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-1\",\n  \"given\": \"An authenticated screen displays protected data and the session expires or a protected request receives HTTP 401\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-1.3\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen returns to sign-in and no protected page data remains displayed\",\n  \"when\": \"The web app handles